rpresser wrote:
 > Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:

 >> The search pattern is not random.  There are
 >> locations far more likely to have a secret door
 >> than others.  Start at the likely locations and
 >> cycle systematically.

 > --SPOILER REQUEST--

 > What are these locations? I've never cottoned on
 > to them; I always search every two spaces along
 > the walls.

Well, to start with, searching includes all eight
grid cells adjacent to the PC, so you only need to
search every _three_ spaces along a wall or corridor
to get complete coverage, since you're searching
_three_ pieces of wall at a time in the normal case.

Next, doors can't be next to other doors, so you can
move one cell further over before searching, you
don't _need_ to search next to a part of the wall
that is ineligible to have a door (unless you're
trying to get complete floor coverage to check for
traps at the same time).

Next, the ends of corridors, or walls facing
vertically or horizontally toward empty space, or
corridors running through space big enough to have a
room on one side or the other, are high priority for
searching, and a room has to be at least four spaces
by four spaces for a vault, including its walls.

Next, walls that are along the absolute edge of the
level grid are a waste of time to search, there's no
room for a corridor.

Next, if you have heard the sound of a vault,
searches toward vault-sized spaces are low priority,
something could be there with no way to reach it.

Next, if you have a pick axe, you can test
horizontally or vertically to learn if the extent of
the level which is in use is smaller than the edge
of the level that is possible. Of course, if you
have a pick axe, or a wand of digging, plowing
through the empty space until you run through a wall
or into a corridor then becomes a viable option; you
can dig through the end of a corridor in seven turns,
finding a secret passage there might take as many as
thirty searches or more, worst case.

If you have good enough armor class not to mind being
lame a lot, and enough food to survive the healing
period, kicking the walls is a low speed way to
search, too.

Next, a stethoscope is much faster to use searching
for doors, one search seems always to suffice, but
now you have to check each grid cell, and each
eligible grid cell on both sides or a corridor
[notice that sides of corridors at the edge of the
level are again not worth searching], because a
stethoscope only checks in the direction you use it.
[This just proved especially useful for me in Mines
End.]

When all else fails, corridors do sometimes start
out on one side of a room, wrap around the room, and
go the other way, so don't completely neglect walls
facing toward "the busy side" of a level, just leave
them toward the end of your search.

Pretty much, this ends up becoming intuition after a
while: do searching checks several times at each
search point to cut down on walking, check the
_rooms_ one time each on the most likely sides,
check the corridors one time each at the ends, check
the rooms on the less likely sides, check the
corridors along their whole length, lather, rinse,
repeat.

Be aware that corridors frequently start out going
somewhere they never reach, do don't insist in your
mind that a corridor end _must_ be the right answer
and search there until you starve.

Be aware that what seems like a closet is sometimes
the start of a corridor whose second cell is a
secret passage, so late in the game of searching, go
around to check all the closets to see if they've
been fooling you as to their real status.

When frustration is really setting in searching, put
your hands in your lap, study the part of the level
you know, review what you've done, and see, cell by
cell, if there is _any_ cell you have somehow failed
to search. It's easy to miss one, or to convince
yourself incorrectly that a cell is ineligible to
hold a door.

Remember that stairs are sometimes hidden by objects
on top of them, so you might already have found the
room with the stairs but not seen the stairs because
you haven't stepped on the object. Statues catch me
this way a lot. In absolutely pathological cases,
the stairs might be under a boulder, or the corridor
continue beyond a boulder, so that you have to
strip, drop everything, squeeze into the space, in a
room to find the stairs under the boulder, in a
corridor to search to find a door or secret passage
beyond the boulder, or even boulders.

